# /libvox-sync configuration. Copy to .env and edit.

# --- Discovery Index --------------------------------------------------------

# Tenant data. Must be an UNPRIVILEGED role: a superuser bypasses row-level
# security, which silently disables tenant isolation.
LIBVOX_DB_DSN=postgresql://libvox_app:libvox_app@127.0.0.1:5435/libvox_index

# Migrations only — this one needs to create objects.
LIBVOX_DB_ADMIN_DSN=postgresql://libvox:libvox@127.0.0.1:5435/libvox_index

LIBVOX_DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
LIBVOX_LOG_LEVEL=INFO

# --- Credential encryption --------------------------------------------------
#
# Encrypts stored ILS secrets (pgcrypto). Generate with:
#
#     openssl rand -base64 32
#
# Two rules, both absolute:
#   1. Never commit it, and never put it in the database it protects — a key
#      stored next to its own ciphertext is decoration.
#   2. Keep it. Credentials written under one key cannot be read back with
#      another; losing it means re-collecting every library's API key.
#
# LIBVOX_CRYPTO_KEY=

# --- ILS credentials (ad-hoc runs only) -------------------------------------
#
# For multi-tenant use, store credentials in the database instead:
#
#     libvox-sync tenant set --tenant furg --ils koha --url ... --client-id ...
#     libvox-sync extract --tenant furg
#
# The variables below are the one-off path — a single library, no database
# lookup. `tenant set` also reads KOHA_CLIENT_SECRET from here, so it never has
# to appear on a command line: /proc/<pid>/cmdline is readable by any local
# process, and shell history keeps it afterwards.
#
# Either way this is standalone mode. In production the credential is resolved
# by /libvox-core, the only component that should decrypt a library's secrets.
#
# KOHA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8081
# KOHA_CLIENT_ID=
# KOHA_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The Tenant DB -- a different database, owned by /libvox-portal, where
# per-tenant ILS credentials live. Nothing here joins across the two, so this
# costs one extra connection and no query rewriting.
#
# Bring it up from there: cd ../libvox-portal && docker compose up -d postgres
LIBVOX_TENANT_DB_DSN=postgresql://libvox_app:libvox_app@127.0.0.1:5435/libvox_tenant

# Cross-tenant inventory only (`tenant list`), which must bypass row-level
# security. Nothing else may use it.
LIBVOX_TENANT_DB_ADMIN_DSN=postgresql://libvox:libvox@127.0.0.1:5435/libvox_tenant

# Inside a container, 127.0.0.1 is the container. Same pattern the Koha driver
# already uses for a host-side Koha.
# LIBVOX_TENANT_DB_DSN_DOCKER=postgresql://libvox_app:libvox_app@postgres:5432/libvox_tenant

# Decrypts the ILS credentials /libvox-portal stored, and must be byte-identical
# to that module's.
#
# The CLI needs it only for `full --tenant` and `extract --tenant`, so it is not
# required to start. **The worker refuses to start without it**: every job it
# runs decrypts a library's credentials, and a worker that started anyway would
# consume each request and fail it -- losing the request and re-disabling the
# button for nothing.
#
# Put it here rather than exporting it: compose reads this file, so the worker
# starts without anyone remembering a shell variable. `./worker.sh` copies the
# portal's key in if this is empty, and refuses to start if it differs -- a key
# that merely exists gets the worker running and then fails every import.
LIBVOX_CRYPTO_KEY=

# --- The embedding half of the search index ---------------------------------
#
# What gives the index its vectors: the worker embeds each record as it
# imports, so a question in one language can reach a shelf catalogued in
# another. See /libvox-core/.env.example for the whole reasoning.
#
# **Commented out on purpose: unset is a supported state.** Nothing here means
# the index is built without vectors and the lexical arm answers alone. Turning
# it on needs a reimport of each collection -- roughly three minutes per nine
# thousand records -- because the column arrives empty.
#
# **All three, unlike Core.** Core falls back to its own LIBVOX_LLM_* settings
# for the gateway and the key; this module has none to fall back to, and a
# model named without somewhere to send it is the same as nothing at all.
# LIBVOX_EMBEDDING_BASE_URL=https://your-gateway.example/v1
# LIBVOX_EMBEDDING_API_KEY=
# LIBVOX_EMBEDDING_MODEL=qwen3-embedding:4b
